---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import RespirationSim from '../../simulators/respiration/RespirationSim.astro';
import { respirationQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/respiration';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What is cellular respiration?' },
  { id: 'atp', label: 'ATP: the cell’s energy currency' },
  { id: 'where', label: 'Where it happens' },
  { id: 'stages', label: 'The four stages' },
  { id: 'oxygen', label: 'What oxygen does' },
  { id: 'anaerobic', label: 'Anaerobic respiration' },
  { id: 'formulas', label: 'Equations and ATP yield' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Chapter 7: Cellular Respiration. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-introduction' },
  { text: 'Berg, J. M., Tymoczko, J. L., Gatto, G. J., & Stryer, L. (2019). Biochemistry (9th ed.), Chapters 16–18. W. H. Freeman.' },
  { text: 'Rich, P. R. (2003). The molecular machinery of Keilin’s respiratory chain. Biochemical Society Transactions, 31(6), 1095–1105.' },
  { text: 'Hinkle, P. C. (2005). P/O ratios of mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation. Biochimica et Biophysica Acta, 1706(1–2), 1–11.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="biology"
  topic="cellular-respiration"
  description="Interactive cellular respiration simulator: watch glucose become ATP in the cytoplasm and mitochondria, step through glycolysis, the Krebs cycle and the electron transport chain, and compare aerobic and anaerobic respiration."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={respirationQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Every heartbeat, thought and step needs energy, and that energy comes from the food you eat. Inside every cell,
      cellular respiration breaks glucose down in a series of small steps and captures the energy in ATP. Watch it happen
      in a living cell, follow each stage, and see what happens when oxygen runs short.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <RespirationSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Inside the cell:</strong> press <strong>Start</strong>. Glucose (yellow hexagons) enters, splits into pyruvate (triangles) and releases ATP. Click the cell parts and the large mitochondrion to learn their roles.</li>
      <li>Lower <strong>Oxygen available</strong> or try the <strong>Sprinting muscle</strong> and <strong>No oxygen</strong> buttons. Watch the ATP bar, lactate and the graph, and compare the ATP made per glucose.</li>
      <li>Change <strong>Glucose available</strong> and the <strong>energy demand</strong> to see what limits ATP production.</li>
      <li><strong>Stage by stage:</strong> use <strong>Previous</strong> and <strong>Next</strong> to follow glycolysis, the link reaction, the Krebs cycle and the electron transport chain, with a running tally per glucose.</li>
      <li><strong>Aerobic vs anaerobic:</strong> compare the ATP yield and products, for animal muscle and for yeast.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">What is cellular respiration?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cellular respiration</strong> is the set of chemical reactions that transfers energy from glucose and other
    food molecules to <strong>ATP</strong>. It happens continuously in almost every living cell, in plants as well as
    animals. It is not the same as breathing: breathing moves air in and out of the lungs to supply oxygen and remove
    carbon dioxide, while respiration is the chemistry inside cells that uses that oxygen.
  </p>

  <h2 id="atp">ATP: the cell’s energy currency</h2>
  <p>
    Glucose holds a lot of energy, but cells cannot use it directly. Instead, the energy is passed in small amounts to
    <strong>adenosine triphosphate (ATP)</strong>. When ATP is broken down to ADP and phosphate, it releases energy exactly
    where it is needed: to contract muscles, pump ions across membranes, build proteins and DNA, and send nerve impulses.
    A cell recycles its ATP thousands of times a day, so respiration must never stop.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where">Where it happens</h2>
  <p>
    The first stage, <strong>glycolysis</strong>, takes place in the <strong>cytoplasm</strong>. The later stages take
    place in the <strong>mitochondria</strong>. A mitochondrion has a smooth outer membrane and a highly folded inner
    membrane; the folds, called <strong>cristae</strong>, provide a large surface for the proteins of the electron
    transport chain. The fluid inside is the <strong>matrix</strong>. Active cells such as muscle and liver cells contain
    hundreds or thousands of mitochondria.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stages">The four stages</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Glycolysis</strong> (cytoplasm): glucose (6 carbons) is split into two pyruvate (3 carbons each). It uses 2 ATP and makes 4, a net gain of 2 ATP, plus 2 NADH.</li>
    <li><strong>Link reaction</strong> (matrix): each pyruvate loses a carbon as CO₂, and the remaining acetyl group joins coenzyme A to form acetyl-CoA. NADH is made.</li>
    <li><strong>Krebs cycle</strong> (matrix): acetyl-CoA joins a 4-carbon molecule to make citrate. As the cycle turns, CO₂ is released and hydrogen is passed to the carriers NAD⁺ and FAD. It turns twice per glucose, making 2 ATP in total.</li>
    <li><strong>Electron transport chain</strong> (inner membrane): NADH and FADH₂ deliver electrons to a chain of proteins. Their energy pumps H⁺ ions into the intermembrane space. The ions flow back through <strong>ATP synthase</strong>, which turns like a tiny motor and makes most of the cell’s ATP.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="oxygen">What oxygen does</h2>
  <p>
    Oxygen is needed only at the very end. It is the <strong>final electron acceptor</strong>: it combines with electrons
    and H⁺ ions to form water. Without oxygen the chain backs up, NADH cannot be turned back into NAD⁺, and the link
    reaction and Krebs cycle stop as well. This is why oxygen is so important even though it is used in just one step.
  </p>

  <h2 id="anaerobic">Anaerobic respiration</h2>
  <p>
    When oxygen is short, cells can still run glycolysis, but they need a way to regenerate NAD⁺. In
    <strong>lactic acid fermentation</strong>, used by animal muscle cells during intense exercise, pyruvate is turned
    into lactate. In <strong>alcoholic fermentation</strong>, used by yeast, it becomes ethanol and carbon dioxide. Both
    give only <strong>2 ATP per glucose</strong>, so cells must use glucose much faster. After exercise, extra oxygen is
    used to deal with the lactate, which is one reason you keep breathing hard after you stop.
  </p>

  <h2 id="formulas">Equations and ATP yield</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Aerobic: <strong>C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6 O₂ → 6 CO₂ + 6 H₂O</strong>, about <strong>30–32 ATP</strong> per glucose.</li>
    <li>Lactic acid fermentation: <strong>C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2 C₃H₆O₃</strong> (lactic acid; lactate in cells), 2 ATP.</li>
    <li>Alcoholic fermentation: <strong>C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2 C₂H₅OH + 2 CO₂</strong>, 2 ATP.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Older textbooks give 36–38 ATP per glucose. Modern measurements give lower values because the H⁺ gradient leaks
    slightly and moving molecules into the mitochondria costs energy. The yield is an average, not a fixed whole number.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>ATP</dt>
    <dd>Adenosine triphosphate, the molecule cells use to transfer energy.</dd>
    <dt>Pyruvate</dt>
    <dd>The 3-carbon product of glycolysis.</dd>
    <dt>NAD⁺ / NADH</dt>
    <dd>A carrier that picks up hydrogen and electrons (NADH) and delivers them to the electron transport chain.</dd>
    <dt>Mitochondrion</dt>
    <dd>The organelle where aerobic respiration is completed.</dd>
    <dt>Fermentation</dt>
    <dd>Anaerobic pathways that regenerate NAD⁺ so glycolysis can continue.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <p>
    Athletes train to deliver oxygen to muscles faster and to tolerate lactate. Bakers rely on yeast fermentation to make
    bread rise, and brewers to make alcohol. Some poisons, such as cyanide, block the electron transport chain, which is
    why they are so dangerous. Many inherited conditions affect mitochondria, especially in tissues with high energy needs
    like muscle and brain.
  </p>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Respiration means breathing.”</strong> Breathing supplies oxygen; respiration is the chemistry in cells.</li>
    <li><strong>“Plants photosynthesise instead of respiring.”</strong> Plants respire all the time, day and night.</li>
    <li><strong>“Respiration makes energy.”</strong> Energy cannot be created; respiration transfers it from glucose to ATP.</li>
    <li><strong>“Lactate causes the muscle soreness felt days later.”</strong> Lactate is cleared within hours; later soreness has other causes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Respiration transfers energy from glucose to ATP in every living cell.</li>
    <li>Glycolysis happens in the cytoplasm; the link reaction, Krebs cycle and electron transport chain happen in mitochondria.</li>
    <li>Oxygen is the final electron acceptor; without it only glycolysis can continue.</li>
    <li>Aerobic respiration gives about 30–32 ATP per glucose; fermentation gives only 2.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">
    Model note: the simulator is a simplified teaching model. Real respiration involves around 20 enzyme-catalysed
    reactions, several shuttles and transport proteins, and tight regulation. Rates, oxygen and glucose levels, and lactate
    are shown in relative units, not measured values, and ATP per glucose uses rounded textbook figures.
  </p>
</SimulatorLayout>
